WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.495
An RTX 3090 has twenty-four gigabytes, and no FP8 tensor cores.

00:00:04.495 --> 00:00:08.126
Which open image and video models still fit on it?

00:00:10.582 --> 00:00:11.489
Hi, I'm Marisol!

00:00:11.489 --> 00:00:15.118
I gave four open models the same brief, a two-storey beach house

00:00:15.118 --> 00:00:18.747
on a beach, and timed every one of them on a 3090.

00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:21.344
Chapter one.

00:00:21.344 --> 00:00:22.411
A weights problem.

00:00:27.508 --> 00:00:28.584
Sum every shard.

00:00:28.584 --> 00:00:31.812
The small FLUX is fourteen point eight seven gibibytes.

00:00:31.812 --> 00:00:33.605
Z-Image, nineteen point one one.

00:00:33.605 --> 00:00:36.115
The video model, twenty-three point oh eight.

00:00:36.115 --> 00:00:40.061
And Qwen-Image two point one is thirty point two one, more

00:00:40.061 --> 00:00:41.495
than the card holds.

00:00:43.617 --> 00:00:47.853
Here is Qwen-Image two point one in host memory: a text encoder,

00:00:47.853 --> 00:00:49.618
a denoiser, and an autoencoder.

00:00:50.038 --> 00:00:53.023
Put all of it on the card and it spills over the

00:00:53.023 --> 00:00:54.267
line before a single activation.

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:56.840
So run one stage at a time.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:00.530
The text encoder comes on alone, reads the prompt once, and leaves.

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:03.961
Then the denoiser comes on and runs its forty steps, with

00:01:03.961 --> 00:01:05.056
plenty of room left.

00:01:05.476 --> 00:01:09.130
The biggest part of the pipeline did its whole job in the first second.

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:10.957
That is why offloading here is cheap.

00:01:12.758 --> 00:01:13.518
Chapter two.

00:01:13.518 --> 00:01:15.037
What Ampere can multiply.

00:01:20.483 --> 00:01:26.548
The 3090's tensor cores take BF16, FP16, TF32 and INT8.

00:01:26.548 --> 00:01:30.793
BF16 ran at seventy-five point five teraflops.

00:01:31.213 --> 00:01:32.914
FP8 arrived a generation later.

00:01:32.914 --> 00:01:37.335
The dtype allocates, but the FP8 matmul raises an error on this card.

00:01:37.335 --> 00:01:40.397
You can store in it, not multiply in it.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.730
That is about fifty-six teraflops, three quarters of a bare matmul.

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:47.471
The card is already busy.

00:01:47.471 --> 00:01:49.560
Fewer bits cannot make it faster.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:54.392
Each step, every one of the small FLUX's three point eight eight billion parameters

00:01:54.812 --> 00:01:58.000
meets each of four thousand and ninety-six image tokens,

00:01:58.420 --> 00:01:59.769
at two FLOPs each.

00:01:59.769 --> 00:02:04.152
Thirty-one point seven teraflops, and the step took point five six five seconds.

00:02:05.633 --> 00:02:09.787
The small FLUX in BF16, seed eleven: two point six seconds.

00:02:10.207 --> 00:02:13.412
The same seed with the denoiser stored in FP8.

00:02:13.412 --> 00:02:17.684
Three point six gibibytes less, five percent slower, and the chimney moved.

00:02:18.104 --> 00:02:22.200
And at two thousand and forty-eight pixels, eighteen point six seconds, with

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.272
four hundred and eighty-two mebibytes left on the board.

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:27.687
Chapter three.

00:02:27.687 --> 00:02:29.278
Five seconds of it moving.

00:02:34.425 --> 00:02:39.154
Start from the small FLUX's golden-hour still, fifteen thirty-six by eight sixty-four.

00:02:39.574 --> 00:02:43.742
The video model resizes it to its own frame, eight sixty-four by four eighty.

00:02:44.162 --> 00:02:46.126
This is the setting that matters.

00:02:46.126 --> 00:02:50.381
With the default scheme, the clip forgot the image after one noisy frame.

00:02:50.381 --> 00:02:51.691
Tail conditioning keeps it.

00:02:52.111 --> 00:02:56.497
Ten steps at thirteen point seven eight seconds each, with module offload

00:02:56.497 --> 00:02:59.421
holding forty-seven point three gibibytes of host memory.

00:02:59.841 --> 00:03:01.878
A hundred and twenty-one frames.

00:03:01.878 --> 00:03:04.322
Three minutes fifty-one seconds, load included.

00:03:06.342 --> 00:03:07.488
And here it is.

00:03:07.488 --> 00:03:11.211
The shingles, the balcony and the boardwalk hold while the camera moves in.

00:03:12.608 --> 00:03:14.752
Here is what broke, and each one is fixable.

00:03:15.172 --> 00:03:19.639
Image to video ignored the image, even the repository's own example, on

00:03:19.639 --> 00:03:21.128
the default conditioning scheme.

00:03:21.128 --> 00:03:23.734
Tail conditioning fixed it for my stills.

00:03:24.154 --> 00:03:28.059
Z-Image finished all nine steps at two thousand and forty-eight pixels, then

00:03:28.059 --> 00:03:30.337
ran out of memory in the decoder.

00:03:30.757 --> 00:03:35.748
And the FP8 matmul simply refused to run on compute capability eight point six.

00:03:37.558 --> 00:03:40.636
On a 3090, precision buys memory, never speed.

00:03:40.636 --> 00:03:44.098
If the BF16 weights fit, run them in BF16.

00:03:44.098 --> 00:03:47.560
If they don't, offload one stage at a time.

00:03:49.025 --> 00:03:49.675
To recap.

00:03:49.675 --> 00:03:53.248
The small FLUX makes an image in two point six seconds.

00:03:53.248 --> 00:03:55.198
FP8 is only storage on Ampere.

00:03:55.198 --> 00:03:58.771
And the video model needs tail conditioning to keep your still.

00:03:59.191 --> 00:04:01.379
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:01.379 --> 00:04:02.004
I'm Marisol.

00:04:02.004 --> 00:04:02.316
Bye!
